1. Accepted Descriptive Name (Common / Working Name)

Hexa-N-nitro hexaazapolyadamantane cage

This is not IUPAC, but it is acceptable as a research or descriptive name

It clearly communicates:

a rigid adamantane-like cage

six ring nitrogens

six N-nitro (nitramine) substituents

2. Correct Systematic IUPAC-Style Name
Full IUPAC Name

Hexakis(N-nitro)-1,3,5,7,9,11-hexaazatricyclo[3.3.1.1³,⁷]decane

This is the correct and defensible IUPAC systematic nomenclature for the structure you described.

3. Breakdown of the IUPAC Name (Explained)
tricyclo[3.3.1.1³,⁷]decane

Describes a 10-atom rigid cage framework

This is the same cage topology as adamantane

“decane” refers to 10 skeletal atoms, not a hydrocarbon

hexaaza-

Six of the ten skeletal atoms are nitrogen

Replaces six carbons of the parent cage

This converts the structure from a hydrocarbon to a heteropolycyclic cage

1,3,5,7,9,11-

Specifies the positions of the six ring nitrogens

Numbering follows heteroatom-priority IUPAC rules

Adamantane numbering is not reused

hexakis(N-nitro)

“hexakis” = six identical substituents

“N-nitro” specifies that each –NO₂ group is bonded to nitrogen

This is nitramine substitution, not carbon-nitro

Using “nitro” alone would be incorrect

4. Why “adamantane” Is NOT in the IUPAC Name

Adamantane is a hydrocarbon parent

Once heteroatoms (nitrogen) are introduced:

adamantane can only be referenced topologically

it cannot be used as the parent name

IUPAC requires polycyclic heterocycle nomenclature instead

This is why tricyclo[3.3.1.1³,⁷]decane is used.

5. Correct Molecular Identity (Summary)

Structural class: Polyaza polycyclic cage

Cage atoms: 4 carbon, 6 nitrogen

Substitution: Six N-nitro (–N–NO₂) groups

Bonding: All nitro groups are on nitrogen (nitramine)

Molecular formula: C₄H₄N₁₂O₁₂

6. Final Clean Statement (You Can Reuse This)

The compound is correctly described as Hexakis(N-nitro)-1,3,5,7,9,11-hexaazatricyclo[3.3.1.1³,⁷]decane, a fully N-nitro-substituted hexaaza cage compound with an adamantane-like topology. The structure consists of a rigid ten-vertex polycyclic framework containing six ring nitrogens and four carbons, with each ring nitrogen bearing one N-nitro (nitramine) substituent.
